The original used a 128 KB lookup table. This version uses a 1024-entry table of message pointers (8 KB), which stays in cache much better.

- **Common case:** profile message numbers are normally below 1024, so they are answered with one indexed load.
- **Larger numbers:** these fall back to a linear scan that returns the first match. That is the same result the original table gave, and a missing number still returns NULL.
- **Not measured:** I have not timed this against the 0.918 baseline or the 0.912 best.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { TABLE_SIZE = 1024 };
    static const Profile::MESG* table[TABLE_SIZE];
    static const bool initialized = []() {
        for (int k = 0; k < TABLE_SIZE; k++)
            table[k] = NULL;
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
            if (n < TABLE_SIZE && table[n] == NULL)
                table[n] = &mesgs[i];
        }
        return true;
    }();
    (void)initialized;

    if (num < TABLE_SIZE)
        return table[num];

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }
    return NULL;
}
```